ct the torsion angles for all  the atoms in the side chain  which ultimately leads to the 3d  coordinates  for every atom in the protein structure  and voila that's it  that's the full network architecture of  alpha fold  2. now let's take a look at how this  beast was  actually trained so alpha fold was  trained using a combination of  four different loss components the most  important of which  is what's called the frame aligned point  error or  vape loss now what fape does is it  essentially compares the  atom coordinates for the predicted  structure with the ground truth  structure  but it does this many times over using  a different alignment for every local  residue frame  and this has the effect of putting extra  importance on getting  locally consistent structure for every  residue  rather than only focusing on the global  coordinate accuracy  the idea here is that it's very very  hard to get all the atom coordinates  perfectly right and more often than not  you want the model to focus on correct  substructures first  and only then start worrying about  global placements  now aside from this fape loss there are  a number of auxiliary losses  first the final pair representation is  linearly projected  to a distance distribution scored with a  cross entropy loss  so here the evo former stack is being  supervised by the target distrogram  second random masking is applied to the  input msas and the network is  tasked to reconstruct those mass regions  using the output msa representations  with  a loss very similar to a model like bert  so here the tricks from unsupervised  language pre-training have been applied  to the input msa stack  which essentially forces alpha fold to  learn  about the evolutionary relationships  between the sequences  in the msa third the single  representations at the output of the  structure module  are used to predict per residue lddt  scores what this means  is that alpha fold tries to predict how  well it thinks it's modeling the  p