ein that we're trying  to predict  all right so what is a distagram well  a distagram is very simply a 2d array of  distances between  every pair of residues in the protein so  at row i and column j  you will find the distance between amino  acid i and amino acid j  in the 3d shape of the folded protein so  essentially a distrogram is a  two-dimensional projection  of the 3d shape of a protein and  since the distance from i to j is the  same as from j to i  the real distrogram well that will  always be a symmetrical  matrix around the main diagonal now why  would you use a distrogram instead of  directly predicting the 3d coordinates  you might ask you know why take this  intermediate step  well there's actually a lot of reasons  someone might want to do this but one  simple and important one is that the  distagram  is invariant to rotations and  translations of the protein  so if you take the 3d structure and you  move it around and rotate it  well the relative distances between the  amino acids stay the same  and so the distrogram is the same so  this makes your target much  easier to learn since you don't have to  figure out some  canonical way of aligning the protein  every time you do a structure prediction  and now rather than trying to directly  predict the target distrogram  alpha fold actually predicts a  distribution over distances  so for every pixel in this 2d matrix  alpha fold will predict  a distribution over 64 possible distance  bins  which is incredibly useful because it  provides an  estimate of how certain the model is  about the distance between two  particular residues  and then finally to get from this  predicted distrogram distribution to the  final 3d shape  alpha fold 1 used a gradient descent  approach  essentially a physical model of the  protein chain is constructed  which is parameterized by the phi and  psi angles  between every two consecutive residues  and  this backbone geometry is then  iteratively refined  using a scoring function that combines  the predictions of the neural net  with a set of physical simulated forces  to figure out the most likely protein  conformation  according to the model now i know that i  skipped over a lot of details here but  that's the essence of alpha fold one  you first collect a multiple sequence  alignment you then predict the  distrogram using a resnet  and then you optimize the protein  backbone using the predicted distrogram  in combination  with a set of physical forces so now  let's transition to the updated version  alpha fold 2.  and now while deepmind's alpha fold has  definitely been at the forefront of  protein structure prediction for the  past few years  i can't make this video without at least  mentioning some of the other amazing  effort in this space some examples and i  can't mention everyone include  the amazing open source work by the  baker lab at the university of  washington  in seattle and more recently some  incredible efforts to replicate the  advances of alpha fold  by the open source community and while  deepmind refrained from publishing their  paper for  almost a year after first announcing  alphavote ii  it's very likely that they were feeling  the building pressure of these ongoing  efforts breathing down their necks  and almost forcing their hand into  publishing their amazing work  before getting scooped by the broader  community because a lot of these ideas  were also being explored by other groups  so this is another  great example of how a lively open  source community  is a really good safeguard against  building monopolies  all right so now that we've set the  stage it's time to dive into the machine  learning architecture  of alpha fall 2. hold on to your hats  because it's about to get a little  technical  so alpha fall 2 consists of three  essential building blocks  the first part of the architecture is  the pre-processing stage  where the input sequence is used to  query additional information from  external databases  this information is then mapped into an  msa and a pair representation  which are refined by what's called the  evo  former a 48-layer deep transformer-like  network  that uses attention mechanisms to update  the msa  and pair representations the outputs of  the evo former  are then processed by the third  component of alpha fold  called the structure module which  basically transforms  the abstract representations formed by  the evil former  into concrete three-dimensional  coordinates  of the protein geometry and with that  overview in mind  let's take a look at each of these  components separately  so given an input chain of amino acids  alpha fold  first creates a multiple sequence  alignment just like in the first version  by searching for similar evolutionary  sequences in a genetic database  additionally though alpha fall 2 will  also search for  structural templates which are known  protein structures  that have similar sequence parts as the  query sequence  now during training these structural  templates are sampled in such a way that  the model  sometimes receives very bad templates or  no templates at all  which has the effect that the network  cannot just rely on copying  a good input template to get an output  structure but it really is  forced to learn from the sequence  information instead  alpha fold then integrates all this  information from the input sequence the  multiple sequence alignment and the  template structures  into a pair representation and an msa  representation  which will be used for further  refinement  in fact because there is so much  external data available  this pre-processing pipeline is usually  repeated a few times  leading to different versions of the  pair and msa representations  which can then be averaged together  before flowing  into the structure module so once the  msa and pair representations are formed  they are forwarded to the  evo former the evil former is a 48-layer  deep network  that contains two separate flows of  information one for the msa  representation on top and another flow  of information  for the pair representation at the  bottom all these 48  blocks have individual parameters so  this is not a recurrent network unlike  the structure module that we'll discuss  in a bit  each block has an msa and a pair  representation as  input as well as its output and it  processes them with several layers  now the layers inside the evil former  well they actually have a lot of  very clever technical tricks that make  it particularly  suitable for updating the protein  representations coming from the input  stage  and the main idea here is to allow the  network to iteratively refine the  information  from the msa and pair representations by  allowing the two modalities to exchange  information  which essentially enables direct  reasoning  about the spatial and the evolutionary  relationships  in the protein so to translate this in  plain english the idea  is that the multiple sequence alignment  contains information about the  evolutionary trajectory  of mutations for the particular protein  in question  but in order to extract all the relevant  information from this msa  well you would have to know something  about the structure that you're dealing  with  and this is what the pair representation  tries to do so in essence these two  flows of information  are just interacting together to solve  an evolutionary puzzle  about the input protein now i'm not  going to go into all the technical  details of how exactly the evil former  does this since there's just  way too much stuff and you really need  to read the supplementary to the paper  if you want to dive deep in the rabbit  hole  but very briefly the msa stack uses an  attention schema called  actual attention whereby alternating  layers  apply row and column attention  sequentially  the idea being that the row wise  attention  allows the model to reason about  information  from each of the aligned sequence  separately while the column attention  lets the model reason about  relationships between the aligned  residues  in different sequences of the msa stack  additionally the row attention is  influenced by a branch coming from the  bottom  allowing the information from the pair  representations to flow  into the msa stack likewise another  branch of the network goes back down to  the bottom flow  to enable an update of the pair  representations  now interestingly this operation is  applied within  every one of the 48 blocks in the evil  former  enabling continuous communication  between the evolving  msa and pair representations now the  pair representations themselves  are updated using another very specific  mechanism  that essentially tries to make the 2d  representations  consistent with the three-dimensional  structure that they represent  and to understand what's happening here  consider that the purpose of these 2d  representations  is to encode things like inter-residue  distances  but there's a bit of a problem here  remember that the feature vector at  position i  j should encode the distance between  residues i  and j so now imagine creating a triangle  in 3d space  going from i to j the k now because  these  two-dimensional features are the output  of the neural net  well it's very likely that the predicted  distances in the 2d feature map  are actually incompatible with any  possible physical triangle  in 3d space and this is why this part of  the network  allows the 2d feature map to update  itself  in such a way that it becomes more  consistent  with an actual three-dimensional  structure again  if you want to learn how exactly alpha  fold does this you should definitely  check out the paper because there's some  very clever tricks  here but so that's the general idea  behind the evil former to gradually  mix information between the msa and the  pair representations  to prepare for structure generation in  the structure module  so the final piece of alpha fold is the  structure module  now this module transforms the abstract  representations that come from the evil  former stack  into a concrete three-dimensional  protein geometry  it does this by passing the first row of  the final msa representation from the  evil former  through eight network layers that all  share the same weights so this is  essentially a recurrent network  that iteratively refines the spatial  geometry of the protein  evoformer's pair representation is also  used in this process  now the structure module operates on a  concrete  three-dimensional backbone which is  represented  as a set of independent rotations and  translations  for each residue with respect to a  global frame of reference  so this is actually pretty fascinating  because it completely ignores the  fundamental constraints  that we know to be present in nature and  yet it appears to work  really really well now in the first  iteration of the structure module  the 3d coordinates are not yet known and  so everything is initialized  to an identity transform which the  authors  fittingly call the black hole  initialization it simply means  that at the start of the structure  module all the residues in the protein  are located at the exact same point  which is the origin of the global frame  and they also have  the exact same orientation now after  this initial  black hole initialization the residue  gas  is then updated iteratively in two  stages  first a novel geometry aware attention  operation called  invariant point attention or ipa  is used to update the sequence  representation without changing the 3d  positions  then an equivariant update is performed  on the residue gas using this updated  sequence representation  now again the invariant point attention  mechanism involves a whole bunch of very  clever tricks  that were specifically designed for the  purpose of protein structure refinement  in a nutshell the invariant point of  tension augments information  coming from the local frame of each  residue such that the activations  are invariant to rotations and  translations in the global frame  so this idea is very related to all the  recent efforts around spatial equivalent  transformers and graph neural networks  if you want to learn more about this i'd  highly recommend  this blog post from fabian about  equivariance  in machine learning it's an amazing read  another interesting note is that  the proposed frame rotations are  represented using something called  quaternions  rather than the usual three by three  rotation matrices  that we're all used to now i suspect  there's a bunch of different reasons for  this  but quaternions actually do provide some  interesting benefits over  regular rotation matrices for example  they can be represented using just  four numbers instead of the nine numbers  that you need  for a three by three rotation matrix  additionally interpolation between  various angles is a perfectly  smooth process where you don't have any  special edge cases like  you have for angles where you know 0  degrees and 360 degrees  is essentially the same angle but it's a  different numerical value  so this could make quaternion  parametrization  much easier to optimize with gradient  descent and finally alpha fold also  fixes the first of the four quaternion  numbers to be  one and what this does is that it biases  the learned rotations  towards very small values which is  really desirable if you have an  iterative updating scheme if you want to  learn more about quaternions  three blue one brown has some amazing  videos on the topic  they're highly recommended so without  going into too much detail here  the main idea is that by iteratively  updating the sequence representation  and the backbone transformations the  black hole initialization of the protein  is gradually adjusted until the final  backbone geometry emerges  and then finally a shallow resnet is  added on top of the final abstract  representations for each residue  to predict the torsion angles for all  the atoms in the side chain  which ultimately leads to the 3d  coordinates  for every atom in the protein structure  and voila that's it  that's the full network architecture of  alpha fold  2. now let's take a look at how this  beast was  actually trained so alpha fold was  trained using a combination of  four different loss components the most  important of which  is what's called the frame aligned point  error or  vape loss now what fape does is it  essentially compares the  atom coordinates for the predicted  structure with the ground truth  structure  but it does this many times over using  a different alignment for every local  residue frame  and this has the effect of putting extra  importance on getting  locally consistent structure for every  residue  rather than only focusing on the global  coordinate accuracy  the idea here is that it's very very  hard to get all the atom coordinates  perfectly right and more often than not  you want the model to focus on correct  substructures first  and only then start worrying about  global placements  now aside from this fape loss there are  a number of auxiliary losses  first the final pair representation is  linearly projected  to a distance distribution scored with a  cross entropy loss  so here the evo former stack is being  supervised by the target distrogram  second random masking is applied to the  input msas and the network is  tasked to reconstruct those mass regions  using the output msa representations  with  a loss very similar to a model like bert  so here the tricks from unsupervised  language pre-training have been applied  to the input msa stack  which essentially forces alpha fold to  learn  about the evolutionary relationships  between the sequences  in the msa third the single  representations at the output of the  structure module  are used to predict per residue lddt  scores what this means  is that alpha fold tries to predict how  well it thinks it's modeling the  position of every residue  and this is a very useful output because  it shows you  where alpha fold is very certain about  its predictions  and where it isn't and finally there are  two  minor auxiliary losses that are used to  better predict  the side chain positions and avoid  structure violations  whereby two atoms would be at the same  place for example  and now the main purpose of all these  different losses is to attach an  individual objective to each major  component of the model  which serves as a guide during training  for the purpose of each subunit  now because the backbone is modeled as a  residue gas there's always a possibility  the network could predict  atom coordinates that clash meaning that  two residues are predicted closer  together than is physically possible  so in order to resolve any remaining  structural violations and clashes  the final predictions are relaxed with a  restrained energy minimization  using the amber force field now towards  the end of the paper there's a very  interesting graph  showing how different elements of the  alpha fold algorithm  affect the final performance of the  model and on this ablation study  you can see a few final tricks that i  haven't mentioned yet  for example alpha sold used something  called self-destination to increase the  size of its training data  essentially alpha fold was first trained  on all  available protein structures in pdb but  then  it predicted the structures of roughly  300 000  additional proteins with unknown  structure  these predicted structures were then  added to the training data but  only the residues that alpha fold  thought it predicted with high precision  were used to perform gradient updates  another trick used  is called recycling whereby outputs of  the model  are recursively fed back into the same  modules  essentially increasing the depth of the  model and giving it more iterations  to refine the predicted protein  structure so  as you can see alpha fold is not the  result  of a single breakthrough idea that  magically solves the protein folding  problem  instead it's a collection of cleverly  designed  and novel ideas that have been improved  over many design cycles  resulting in this amazingly well  engineered system  and there you go that's alpha fall 2 in  all its glory  so to end this video let's briefly zoom  out a little bit and talk about the  bigger picture  i mean is protein folding really solved  now  well yes and no first of all  let me state this very clearly alpha  fold is a tremendous engineering  achievement by some of the most talented  ai researchers in the world  the model is able to predict the  structures of most  single domain proteins in biology with  incredible accuracy  and it's undeniable that this will  herald a completely new era in  computational biology  where ai will be used as an  indispensable tool  for designing new drugs understanding  disease pathways  fighting viruses and much more at the  same time however alpha folds accuracy  isn't always perfect for example many  processes in biology rely on  large protein complexes whereby many  different proteins come together  to form complex molecular machines which  are currently  outside of alpha volt's capabilities  there's also the notion of  generalization i mean  alpha fold was trained on a data set of  proteins that naturally occur in biology  but we don't really know how good its  predictions will generalize  to completely new de novo proteins that  we might want to design with these new  tools  additionally most proteins function by  changing  shape with respect to targets they bind  to and  these molecular dynamics well they're  not captured by  alpha fold's static view of protein  structure  being able to model the flexibility of a  real protein in these different  conformations  will be crucial to study protein protein  interactions  that being said though it's very likely  that the ideas proposed by the alpha  fall team  can be extended to those new challenges  given the right types of training data  and engineering efforts  all in all i think many people will  agree with me when i say that this is  probably  one of the most impactful machine  learning models of this decade  so the fact that deepmind has finally  published their paper and released their  code  including pre-trained model weights is  an incredible gesture towards the  scientific community  in fact we're now living in a world  where you can simply  run alphafall the world's most advanced  protein folding model  directly in your browser using google  colab with zero setup  giving you state of the art structure  predictions for  any protein you're interested in i mean  honestly  that's pretty mind-blowing and with that  i would like to thank you very much for  watching till the end of this video  please consider supporting this channel  on patreon making these videos  is a lot of work check out my pen site  page if you need one-on-one meetings to  discuss your own projects  but most of all say thanks to the  billions of active proteins keeping you  alive right now have an amazing day  [Music]
 

